  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions:\n\nSurface high pressure resides over the region this afternoon with a \nstalled out frontal boundary south of the Northland draped through \nsouthern MN and central WI. Despite the increasing clouds aloft\nthe westerly winds across the region have helped temperatures \nclimb into the upper 70s and mid 80s this afternoon. Winds are \nalso slightly breezy with sensors reporting gusts in excess of \n20 mph. After sunset we can expect winds to taper off and our \novernight lows to cool into the 50s. \n\nWednesday:\n\nTonight and into Wednesday morning there will be a shortwave moving \nacross the Upper Midwest. However, rain chances don't look overly \nimpressive for our neck of the woods. The better chances will be \nfurther south closer to the stationary boundary. Some rain may be \nable to migrate into our southern counties. But looking at sounding \nprofiles there is a significant dry layer below 700 mb that will \ndrastically cut down on rain totals if not completely keep the \nsurface dry. Any activity that does form will quickly move off \nand out of the region by late morning. Cloud coverage will \ndecrease through the remainder of the day with highs in the 70s \nand 80s once again. \n\nLate Week:\n\nAn upper level ridge builds over the Upper Midwest later this week. \nDespite the typical suppression that accompanies this feature, our \nweather pattern looks to turn slightly active. An upper level low \nover the southern Canadian Prairies will once again have an arching \noccluded front with an accompanying warm front through the Northland \non Thursday. This will lead to continued warmth across the region \nwith highs well above normal for this time of year reaching into the \nmid 80s. A resurgence of humidity will also stream back into the \nregion with dewpoints climbing back into the 60s and 70s. \n\nWith the warm and moist air mass lingering across the region we will \nbe keeping an eye on possible triggers for convection. The upper \nlevel ridge overhead will likely mitigate our typical air mass \nthunderstorms, but the aforementioned frontal boundary could provide \nenough forcing for storms to get going. There will also be several \nimpulses aloft rounding the ridge that would aide in storm \ndevelopment. Placement and timing of said features still remains \nuncertain this far out but we will maintain some PoPs of 40-60% for \nthe Thursday and Friday periods. Additionally, it is worth noting \nthat the NCAR family of NWP convective hazards all depict a small \nchance of severe from the northwest corner of MN to SE near the\nBrainerd Lakes Region, which coincides pretty closely to where \nthe deterministic guidance is painting the warm sector of the \napproaching weather system Thursday into Friday.",
